# Cursor Rules - Core Engineering Discipline
# Drop this file in your project root as `.cursorrules`.
# These are generic, language-agnostic rules. Add project-specific rules below.

## Truthfulness & accuracy (most important)
- Never invent APIs, libraries, functions, methods, or config options. If you are
  not sure something exists, say so explicitly instead of guessing.
- If a request is ambiguous or you lack context, ask a clarifying question rather
  than assuming. A wrong assumption is more expensive than a question.
- Do not fabricate file paths, env var names, or data. If you need to see a file,
  ask to read it; do not imagine its contents.
- When you are uncertain, state your confidence level and what would resolve it.

## Read before you write
- Before editing, read the surrounding code and match the project's existing
  patterns, naming conventions, style, and libraries. Do not impose a new style.
- Use the language/framework versions already in the project. Do not introduce a
  new framework, library, or major dependency without flagging it and explaining
  why it's needed.

## Make minimal, focused changes
- Change only what the task requires. Do not refactor, rename, or reformat
  unrelated code unless asked.
- Do not rewrite an entire file when a small edit will do.
- Never delete existing code or functionality unless it's clearly part of the
  task. Preserve working behavior.
- Do not silently change public interfaces (function signatures, API routes,
  schemas). Call it out if a change is necessary.

## Don't fake it
- No placeholder logic, stub data, or "TODO: implement later" unless explicitly
  requested. If you can't complete something, say what's missing and why.
- Do not hardcode values to make a test or demo pass. Solve the real problem.
- If you generate example/mock data, label it clearly as mock.

## Correctness & robustness
- Handle errors and edge cases explicitly. No empty/bare catch blocks that swallow
  errors. Fail loudly with a clear message rather than failing silently.
- Validate inputs at boundaries (user input, file I/O, network, external data).
- Check for the obvious failure modes: null/None, empty collections, division by
  zero, missing files, off-by-one in ranges and indices.
- Prefer clear, simple code over clever code. Readability beats brevity.

## Security
- Never hardcode secrets, API keys, passwords, tokens, or credentials. Read them
  from environment variables or a secrets manager.
- Never put sensitive data in logs, URLs, or query strings.
- Use parameterized queries / prepared statements - never build SQL (or shell
  commands) by string concatenation of user input.
- Don't commit `.env`, credentials, or generated artifacts. Respect `.gitignore`.

## Code quality
- Use type hints / types wherever the language supports them.
- Keep functions small and single-purpose. Avoid deep nesting; prefer early returns.
- Avoid global mutable state. Pass dependencies explicitly.
- Name things for what they are. No single-letter names except trivial loop counters.
- Comments explain *why*, not *what*. Don't comment the obvious; do document
  non-obvious decisions, assumptions, and units (e.g. "value in kPa").

## Testing
- When adding non-trivial logic, add or update tests, or state exactly how to test it.
- Make code testable: pure functions where possible, side effects at the edges.
- Don't claim something works - show how it was or can be verified.

## Communication
- For anything beyond a trivial edit, briefly explain your approach before the code.
- Flag risky or irreversible actions (deleting data, migrations, changing auth,
  rate-limited or paid API calls) and wait for confirmation.
- When you finish, note any assumptions you made and anything left to do.
